#define TRS_TROUBLE_INDEX_LAUNCH        1210
//If you experience any trouble with The DIG, it may be the result of hardware conflicts or improper
//configuration of your computer.  The DIG pushes a computer to its performance limits.  Thus, minor
//conflicts may show themselves when playing The DIG, even if the computer seems to behave
//properly with other software.
//
//The first step in diagnosing a problem running The DIG is to isolate the conditions under which problems
//occur.  Please use this interactive troubleshooting manual to aid in your troubleshooting procedure.
//
//If you are unable to resolve the problem, call technical support at the numbers at the 
//end of this manual, or contact us on-line.
//
//
//^f01 Guide Index^f00
//
//
//
//     ^c001Help Text_________________________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//     ^c001LucasArts Entertainment Contact Information_______________________________________________^c014
//
//
//
//
//
//
//   * All trademarks are hereby acknowledged as the proprietary property of their respective owners.

#define TRS_TROUBLE_MEM        1220
//
//^f01 Memory Issues and Concerns
//^f00
//You should have 8 megabytes of memory or more on your machine to run The DIG
//properly.  The amount of low memory (below 640k) is not important.
//
//The DIG runs in your computer's 386/486 native "protect" mode.  Many programs
//still run in what is called "real" mode, which is a mode 386 and 486 chips can run in to emulate 
//the 8088 chip found in the original IBM PC.  One of the limitations of running in "real" mode 
//is that such programs must fit entirely in a computer's lower 640k of memory, even if a particular 
//machine has many megabytes of total memory.  In addition, such programs cannot access the 
//memory above one megabyte without use of a memory manager such as EMM386 or 386Max.  These 
//utilities exist to allow older "real" mode programs to see memory above one megabyte and also 
//to maximize the amount of low (below 640k) memory that these programs must fit into.
//
//Since The DIG runs in protected mode, it does not care how much "low" memory exists
//because high memory is just as easy to use.  Therefore, it does not need a memory manager to
//run.  In fact, some memory managers allocate all of a system's memory for real-mode emulation,
//leaving none for protected-mode programs to run.  While this feature can often be overridden
//by reconfiguring the memory manager, it is often undesirable to do so because of the 
//following reason:  memory managers slow down access to memory, even if their functionality is
//not being utilized.  Thus, when running The DIG, it's often best to run with no memory manager
//at all.
//
//Since you'll probably want to still run your normal "real" mode programs, the best
//way to disable your memory manager is to start your computer with a boot disk made using the
//"Make Boot Disk" option under the Main Menu of this program.


#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95        1292
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues
//^f00
//The DIG is designed to run under MS-DOS (Your computer says, "Starting MS-DOS," when it 
//starts up as opposed to, "Starting Windows 95.") However, users of Windows 95 can run The DIG
//in several different ways:
//
//1.  Dual-Boot Machines:
//    If your computer is configured to dual-boot, please restart your previous version of MS-DOS 
//    to run The DIG.  If you don't know whether or not you can Dual-Boot, try pressing the F4 key
//    immediately when your computer says, "Starting Windows 95."  If it responds with, "Restarting
//    your Previous version of MS-DOS," then your computer has Dual-Boot capabilities.
//
//2.  MS-DOS Prompt:
//    The DIG can be run through the MS-DOS Prompt by using one of the PIF files provided on The DIG 
//    CD-ROM. If your computer has 16 Megabytes of RAM or more, use the icon labeled:
//    'DIG16MB.'  This is the recommended method for running The DIG on a computer that 
//    has 16 or more Megabytes of RAM.  If your computer has less than 16 Megabytes of RAM, use
//    the icon labeled 'DIG8MB.'   
//
//    Double-click on The DIG icon to start The DIG in this mode.  This runs The DIG in an MS-DOS
//    'Box.' Please note that machines with 8 Megabytes of RAM may suffer some performance problems
//    when running The DIG in this manner.  Please refer to the section, 'Windows 95 Trouble-
//    shooting,' for details.  
//
//3.  MS-DOS Mode:
//    If you are running Windows 95 you can restart your computer in MS-DOS Mode.  Once you are at 
//    the C:\WINDOWS> prompt, switch to your CD-ROM drive and type DIG to launch the program.
//    NOTE:  When restarting in MS-DOS Mode you may experience problems running The DIG if your 
//    computer is configured with memory managers. The first step in solving this problem is to
//    make a boot disk.  To make a boot disk in Windows 95, follow the instructions on the next page.
//    If, after making a boot disk, you still experience problems, please refer to the section 'Windows 95
//    Troubleshooting,' for details.   
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1293
//
//^f01          Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//
//^f01            Making A Boot Disk in Windows 95
//^f00
//
//To make a boot disk for The DIG in Windows 95, follow these instructions.  First, double-click on the
//'My Computer' icon on your desktop (please note:  The name of this icon may have been changed 
//by you or by the person who installed Windows 95 on your computer).  Double click on the CD-ROM
//drive icon in the 'My Computer' window.  This should open a window titled DIGWIN95.  Inside, you 
//should see a folder labeled 'Dig'.  Open this folder, and double click on the 'ALLBOOT' icon.  This
//starts the Boot Disk Maker utility.  Follow the instructions on your screen to make a boot disk.
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1294
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//^f00
//WINDOWS 95 TROUBLESHOOTING: 
//
//MS-DOS Prompt:
//
//If you are running The DIG by double-clicking on the DIG8MB or the DIG16MB icon on The DIG CD-ROM,
//you are running The DIG through your MS-DOS Prompt.  (Please note that this is NOT the same as running
//The DIG under real MS-DOS).  The DIG may suffer some performance problems when running through the
//MS-DOS Prompt, especially if your computer has only 8 Megabytes of RAM.
//
//Why this is true:  
//
//The DIG is an MS-DOS program that requires 8 Megabytes of free RAM to run.  However, when you are
//using Windows 95 on an 8 Megabyte machine you are unlikely to have 8 Megabytes of free RAM.  The Windows
//95 Operating System can consume anywhere from 1.5 to 4 Megabytes of RAM depending on how your
//system is configured.  Therefore, an 8 Megabyte machine running Windows 95 may only have 6.5 to 4
//Megabytes of free RAM at any time.  Because The DIG requires 8 Megabytes of free RAM, Windows 95
//will utilize Virtual Memory to allow The DIG to run even though the amount of actual free RAM is
//less than 8 Megabytes.  Most machines running Windows 95 with 8 Megabytes of RAM will utilize
//Virtual Memory to run The DIG.  Virtual Memory, as the name implies, is not real, physical RAM and is
//much slower than actual RAM.  Because it is so much slower, it slows down gameplay in The DIG.
//
//
//
//
//
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1296
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//^f00
//
//A SPECIAL NOTE ABOUT MACHINES THAT COME WITH WINDOWS 95 PRE-INSTALLED
//
//If your computer came with Windows 95 pre-installed, your computer may have certain aspects
//of its configuration that harm gameplay performance in The DIG.  These configuration options,  
//when set properly, can help boost performance on a computer running The DIG through the MS-DOS
//Prompt.  Please note that changing these configuration options will not affect The DIG when it is run 
//in MS-DOS Mode.  
//
//WARNING:  Changing your system configuration settings may hinder performance of other applications.
//
//If your computer came with a Windows 95 interface program such as the Packard Bell
//Navigator, HP Personal Page, or other utility program that runs on top of the Windows 95
//operating system, you should exit it before you run The DIG.  If you are not sure if you have
//one of these types of programs running on your computer, you can tell in one of several ways.
//If you can not see the Windows Taskbar (the grey bar with the 'Start' button on it) or you can
//not see the standard Windows 95 system icons (the 'My Computer', and 'Recycle Bin' icons)
//when your computer has started up then you are likely running an operating system 'shell' 
//program supplied by the manufacturer of your computer.  These 'shell' programs consume 
//large amounts of memory and should be disabled or exited before running The DIG.
//    
//Many systems set the CD-ROM Read Ahead Cache to Large by default.  This setting can cause 
//stuttering music, sound, and voice,  as well as stuttering video playback during cutscenes in The
//DIG.  If you experience these symptoms, reducing the Windows 95 CD-ROM Read Ahead cache
//to its minimum size can help.  To do this, exit The DIG.  Click on the Start button on your Taskbar.
//Highlight your 'Settings', and select 'Control Panel'.   When your Control Panel window appears, 
//double-click on the 'System' icon.  This will bring up a window titled 'System Properties'.  Select 
//the 'Performance' tab from the top-right corner of the window, then click the 'File System' button
//on the bottom left.  This will bring up a window titled 'File System Properties'.  Click on the 
//'CD-ROM' tab at the top of the window.  Under 'Settings', drag the slider for 'Supplemental cache
//Size' all the way to the left toward 'Small'.  To further optimize these settings for The DIG, you can
//set the 'Optimize Access Pattern for:' setting to 'No read-ahead'.
//
//  
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1295
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//^f00
//
//Below is a list of normal problems encountered when running The DIG through
//the MS-DOS Prompt:
//
//1. Occasional Sound / Voice Stuttering.  (for example, you may hear voices that sound like t-t-thank 
//    you, Boston L-Low).  Further, music may momentarily pause from time to time.  This can happen
//    during normal gameplay, when moving from room to room, and during video cutscenes.
//
//2. Frame Dropping.  (Non-interactive scenes during the game may appear choppy).
//
//3. Slow Frame Rate (Some interactive portions of the game may appear to be in slow motion).
//
//4. Long Load Times (It may take a long time to move from room to room.)  Also, you may experience a 
//   'lag' of one or two seconds between right-clicking to bring up your inventory and when your inventory
//   actually comes up. Further, upon entering a room you may find that the cursor will move around but
//   not be useful to interact with items for one or two seconds
//
//Solutions to the Above Problems:
//If you find that The DIG runs too slow when using the MS-DOS Prompt there is only one way to
//speed it up: increase the available free RAM on your system.  Start by making sure that you
//do not have ANY other programs running when you start The DIG.  If The DIG is still too slow
//to play when no other applications are running, to increase performance further you must reduce
//the amount of memory that Windows 95 itself uses.  This can be accomplished by turning off 
//or disabling system extensions such as, but not limited to, desktop pictures, animated icons,
//event sounds, desktop themes, system activity monitors (such as System Agent), and screen savers.
//If your computer has one or more of the above features installed, turning them off and restarting
//your computer will help free up some memory for The DIG.  If The DIG is still too slow, 
//restarting your computer in MS-DOS mode should allow The DIG to run at full speed.  
//   
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1297
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//^f00
//MS-DOS Mode:
//
//If you are restarting your computer in MS-DOS mode (Please note that this is NOT the same as 
//running The DIG under real MS-DOS) you may experience one of the following problems:
//
//1.	Program Crashes
//	If the game crashes when you try to run it in MS-DOS Mode it is probably because your computer is
//	loading a 16-bit memory manager such as EMM386.  To fix this problem, select the 'Make Boot Disk'
//	option from the launcher menu and re-boot your computer with the boot disk.  If the game crashes
//	before you even get to the launcher screen, run ALLBOOT.EXE.  ALLBOOT.EXE is located in the DIG
//	subdirectory of The DIG CD-ROM.  Reboot your computer with the boot disk in your disk drive and 
//	then run The DIG.
//  
//2.	No CD-ROM Drive Present
//	Computers that came with Windows 95 as their original operating system (usually newer machines that
//	have never had DOS) may not load the drivers necessary for the CD-ROM drive to work in MS-DOS Mode.
//	If your CD-ROM drive is normally D: in Windows 95, but when you restart in MS-DOS mode and type D:
//	you get, 'Invalid Drive Specification,' then this is the problem.  To fix this problem you need to
//	add the DOS drivers for your CD-ROM drive to your CONFIG.SYS file, and MSCDEX.EXE to your 
//	DOSSTART.BAT file.  Please contact your hardware manufacturer for details about locating the proper
//	drivers for your hardware.  An alternative is to try running The DIG in an MS-DOS Box by following 
//	the instructions under, 'WINDOWS 95 ISSUES, MS-DOS Prompt.'
//
// 
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_WIN95_1     1298
//^f01 Windows 95 Compatibility Issues Cont.
//^f00
//MS-DOS Mode:
//
//If you are restarting your computer in MS-DOS mode (Please note that this is NOT the same as 
//running The DIG under real MS-DOS) you may experience one of the following problems (Continued):
//
//3.	Mouse Not Working
//	Computers that came with Windows 95 as their original operating system (usually newer machines that have
//	never had DOS) may not load the drivers necessary for the mouse to work in MS-DOS Mode.  To fix this 
//	problem you need to add the DOS driver for your mouse to your DOSSTART.BAT file.  Please contact your 
//	hardware manufacturer for details about locating the proper drivers for your hardware.  An alternative
//	is to try running The DIG in an MS-DOS Box by following the instructions under, 'WINDOWS 95 ISSUES, MS-
//	DOS Prompt.'
//
//4.	Sound Doesn't Work
//	Computers that came with Windows 95 as their original operating system (usually newer machines that have
//	never had DOS) may not load the drivers necessary for the sound card  to work in MS-DOS Mode.  To fix
//	this problem you need to add the DOS drivers for your sound card to your DOSSTART.BAT file and / or your 
//	AUTOEXEC.BAT file.  Please contact your hardware manufacturer for details about locating the proper
//	drivers for your hardware.  An alternative is to try running The DIG in an MS-DOS Box by following the
//	instructions under, 'WINDOWS 95 ISSUES, MS-DOS Prompt.'
//
//5.	Boot Disk Maker Says "No Config or Autoexec Files"
//	If your computer came with Windows 95 pre-installed and does not load DOS mode drivers at startup time,
//	or, when you upgraded to Windows 95 you erased all of your DOS configuration files, you will not be able
//	to run The DIG in MS-DOS Mode.  If you try to make a Boot Disk on a machine with this configuration you 
//	will get the above error message.  Contact your hardware manufacturer for details about installing 
//	MS-DOS Mode drivers for Windows 95.  An alternative is to try running The DIG in an MS-DOS Box by
//	following the instructions under, 'WINDOWS 95 ISSUES, MS-DOS Prompt.'
//


#define TRS_TROUBLE_CRASH_LAUNCH        1230
//^f01 Game Crash or Lockup Issues
//^f00
//If you are experiencing crashes during any part of The DIG, try one of the following 
//steps to resolve the problem:
//
//* If you have not done so already, make a boot disk using the "Make Boot Disk" option from the main
//  menu of this launcher or by running "ALLBOOT.EXE" from the DIG directory of the The DIG CD.  Reboot 
//  off the boot disk and see if the problem remains.  If it disappears, you most likely have a memory 
//  manager or utility whose operation conflicts with The DIG.
//
//***PLEASE NOTE*** The Boot Disk maker utility will NOT run under Windows 95 if you start it by using the
//  "Make Boot Disk" option from the main menu of this launcher.  To run the Boot Disk maker in Windows 95,
//   follow the instructions in the "Windows 95 Issues" section of this guide.
//
//* If the problem remains, try disabling your sound by using the "Configure Sound" option from the main
//  menu of this launcher.  Type 'N' when prompted for the 'No Sound Card' option. If symptoms disappear,
//  your mouse and/or soundcard most likely conflict with another device in your computer or each other.
//  You will need to consult the documentation that came with your mouse or sound card for specific
//  instructions on how to configure those items so that they do not conflict.
//
//* If you still have problems, select one of the DOS Extender buttons below which will copy an alternate
//  version of  the DOS extender to your disk drive.  Version 1.97 is the default that is copied when you
//  install.  The other versions may be more compatible with some systems.  PLEASE NOTE: To use your
//  new DOS Extender you must first exit to the DOS prompt before running The DIG.     
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_SOUND        1240
//^f01 Sound Issues and Concerns
//^f00
//After selecting the 'Configure Sound Card' option from game launcher utility you will be 
//asked to choose either 'Express Setup' or 'Custom Setup.' If the sound setup program fails to
//configure your sound board automatically then you should choose 'Custom Setup.'  If you have added 
//a new sound card to your system or changed your sound board configuration, then select 
//'Express Setup' and the program will attempt to set up your sound board automatically.
//
//From the 'Custom Setup' Standard Menu, you can choose from the sound standards supported by 
//The DIG and manually set the Port, IRQ, DMA, and Stereo Reverse settings.  You can also 
//test your configuration.
//
//     ^c001Sound Configuration Problems______________________________________________________________^c014
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//
//     ^c001Hardware Specific Issues__________________________________________________________________^c014
//

#define TRS_TROUBLE_IMUSENOCARD        1242
//^f01 No Sound Card Found
//^f00
//
//Check your startup files to be sure that the device drivers for your sound card are being loaded
//correctly.  If not, refer to the documentation for your sound card or contact your sound card 
//manufacturer for instructions.
//
//If you are using Windows 95 and are restarting your computer in MS-DOS Mode, you must make sure that you 
//are loading MS-DOS Mode drivers for your sound card when you start Windows 95.  If your sound card does
//not work properly in MS-DOS Mode, please contact your sound card manufacturer for details on how to 
//properly configure it to run in MS-DOS Mode.
#define TRS_TROUBLE_IMUSEBADDETECT     1243
//^f01 Sound Card Detected Incorrectly
//^f00
//Some Sound Blaster Pro compatible sound cards may not detect properly if set to DMA 0 or DMA 3. You may
//set the values manually using the sound configuration utility.  The Dig's  sound configuration is run 
//when installing the game or when you choose 'Configure Sound Card' from the Main Menu of the launcher.
//When prompted for 'Express' or 'Custom' setup, choose 'Custom' and manually set the DMA to match the 
//setting on your sound card.  This should resolve the problem.
//
//The original Sound Blaster Pro should detect properly on all channels.
//
//If you are using Windows 95 and are restarting your computer in MS-DOS Mode, you must make sure that you 
//are loading MS-DOS Mode drivers for your sound card when you start Windows 95.  If your sound card does
//not work properly in MS-DOS Mode, please contact your sound card manufacturer for details on how to 
//properly configure it to run in MS-DOS Mode.
#define TRS_TROUBLE_IMUSEBADSOUND      1244
//^f01 Poor Sound Quality
//^f00
//If you are experiencing poor sound quality with your Sound Blaster Pro compatible sound card, you may get
//better results if you configure your sound card as 'Sound Blaster 2.0' in the sound configuration utility.  The 
//DIG sound configuration is run when you choose 'Configure Sound Card' from the Main Menu of the game 
//launcher.  The original Sound Blaster Pro card should not have this problem.
//
//If you are running The DIG on an 8 Megabyte computer with the Windows 95 MS-DOS Prompt (by using the 
//DIG8MB icon) you may experience stuttering sound and voice (for example, you may hear voices that sound
//like t-t-thank you, Boston L-Low).  Further, music may momentarily pause from time to time.  Please
//refer to the Windows 95 Issues section of this guide for details.
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_SOUND_HDWR0  1250
//^f01 Sound Card Compatibility
//^f00
//We experienced some problems when testing The DIG with the following sound cards.  You may or may not 
//experience these problems when using this hardware.  We pass this information along for your benefit.
//
//OPTi MAD16 Based sound cards:
//If you experience difficulty running The DIG with this type of card, you can configure it by selecting
//'Configure Sound Card' in the main menu of this launcher.  However, when prompted, select 'Custom
//Setup' by pressing 'C' and manually configure the card.  Choose 'Sound Blaster 2.0' for the type of card.
//DO NOT run the sound tests.  Exit the sound configuration utility and start The DIG. 
//
//Logitech SoundMan 16
//Logitech cards MUST be set up manually as a Sound Blaster 2.0 in The DIG's sound card configuration 
//utility.  The Auto-Detect will express-detect as a Pro Audio Spectrum card.  You may experience 
//problems with this setting.  If you do, try choosing 'Sound Blaster 2.0'.
// 
//Pro Audio Spectrum 16
//This card will have improved performance in The DIG if you configure it with a 16-bit high (5 or above) 
//DMA channel.  Setting a low (8-bit) DMA channel causes degraded sound and music quality.  Please 
//refer to your sound card documentation for details.
//
//Early Sound Blaster Cards (version 1.0 -1.5) and compatibles
//These early versions of the 8-bit Sound Blaster card and other sound cards that emulate a Sound Blaster 
//version 1.0 - 1.5 are unable to support the type of high-definition music used in The DIG.  Using this 
//type of card with The DIG may result in graphic corruptions or other errors, especially on 486 machines.  
//This type of card is NOT supported by The DIG.  Sound Blaster 2.0 and 100% compatible cards should 
//perform well when used with The DIG.
//
//Sound Blaster 16 / AWE-32 / Vibra 16 / SB32
//This card will have improved performance in The DIG if you configure the high DMA channel setting to use
//a 16-bit DMA channel (5 or above).  Setting the high DMA to a low (8-bit) DMA channel causes degraded 
//sound and music quality.  Please refer to your sound card documentation for details.
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_SOUND_HDWR0  1251
//^f01 Sound Card Compatibility Continued
//^f00
//
//Advanced Gravis Ultrasound 
//The DIG may perform poorly when used with this card on 486's or with CD-ROM drives that use
//or exceed 60% of CPU time to maintain a 300k/sec transfer rate.  Problems include slow gameplay, 
//choppy or skipped video sequences, and possible game crashes.  
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_GETUS        1280
