#define TRS_TROUBLE_MISC0       1300
//^f01 Miscellaneous Issues
//^f00
//     ^c001CONFIGURATION FILES_______________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//DIG.EXE, the The DIG setup utility creates one file needed for game play.
//
//This file is called IMUSE.INI and contains sound configuration information.  The file is kept 
//in the C:\DIG directory.  If it is accidentally deleted, simply select the "Configure Sound Card"
//option from the main menu of the game launcher.
//
//     ^c001COMPUTERS_________________________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//If you are running The DIG on a computer that came with Windows 95 you should read the section of
//this guide titled 'Windows 95 Issues' for specific information on how best to run The DIG.
//
//     ^c001CONTROLLERS_______________________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//To use the joystick, it's important to note that you must first calibrate it.  To calibrate the
//joystick, press Ctrl-J while The DIG is running and follow the instructions given.
//
//     ^c001DISPLAY____________________________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//If, after making a Bootdisk and restarting your computer, you find that your display is suddenly
//off center, please readjust the centering of your monitor manually using the standard control knobs.
//
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
#define TRS_TROUBLE_MISC1       1301
//^f01 Miscellaneous Issues Cont.
//^f00
//     ^c001SAVED GAME FILES__________________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//Saving your game while running The DIG creates a saved game file in the C:\DIG directory.
//Each saved game file requires anywhere from  90-350K of hard disk space.
//
//     ^c001SMARTDRIVE / CD-CACHING SOFTWARE__________________________________________________________^c014
//
//CD disk-caching software may confuse The DIG if the CD drive encounters a read error.
//If you are having problems, disable any disk-caching software or caching options of your CD driver.
//This can especially be a problem for Windows 95 users.  See the Windows 95 Issues section of
//this guide for instructions on how to configure the CD-ROM Read Ahead cache in Windows 95.
//
//     ^c001DEDICATED GAME CARDS______________________________________________________________________^c014
//
//Game cards can cause major problems with The DIG.  An improperly adjusted or conflicting game card 
//can cause The DIG to run extremely slow, even on very fast CPU's (Pentium 133).  If you experience
//large slowdowns and you have a game card installed try the following steps to correct the problem:
//
//1.	Unplug any devices from the card.
//2.	If it is an adjustable-speed game card, make sure that it is adjusted properly.
//3.	If all else fails, remove the card from your system.
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_CDROM0 1350
//^f01 CD-ROM Hardware Issues
//^f00
//If you are experiencing some slowdowns while running The DIG, small performance gains
//can be obtained by optimizing your CD-ROM drive's configuration for this type of program.  To 
//optimize your CD-ROM drive's configuration for The DIG, you should reduce the size of your 
//MSCDEX buffer to the minimum value of 4, and be sure that you are running the latest version of the
//driver software for your CD-ROM drive.  The Boot Disk Maker included with The DIG will automatically
//change the MSCDEX buffer to 4 if you make a boot disk.  Please contact your hardware manufacturer
//for the latest CD-ROM drivers for your CD-ROM drive.  
//
//Remember that The DIG requires a minimum 486DX2-66 CPU, in addition to an MPC-Level 2 CD-ROM drive.
//Keep in mind that anything that exceeds roughly 60% of the CPU on a 486DX2-66, will most likely have
//performance problems with The DIG.  In addition, while the MPC-Level 2 standard has a minimum CPU
//of 486DX-33, The DIG requires a 486DX2-66 minimum CPU to run properly.  
//^c001Definition of MPC Level 2^c014
//
//  Short Version:
//MPC Level 2 means that a CD-ROM can spool data to the CPU at a sustained rate of 300K/second
//while using less than 60% of the CPU.
//
//  Long Version:
//MPC Level 2 specifies a minimum of a 25 MHz 486 CPU with 4 Megabytes of RAM and 160 Megabytes of
//available hard disk space.  CD-ROMs for MPC Level 2 must be capable of a sustained rate of at least
//300K/second and must have an access time of 400 milliseconds or less.  An MPC Level 2 CD must also
//use no more than 60% of the CPU's resources during a 300K/second read and no more than 40% at
//150K/second throughput.
//
//
//                                             ^c001More Next Page^c014
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_CDROM1 1351
//^f01 CD-ROM Compatibility Cont.
//^f00
//  
//When we tested The DIG with the following equipment, we experienced some difficulty with certain
//hardware combinations.  You may or may not experience the same problems.  We pass this information
//along for your benefit.
//
//Keep in mind that anything that exceeds roughly 60% of the CPU on a 486DX2-66, will most likely have
//performance problems with The DIG.  In addition, while the MPC-Level 2 standard has a minimum CPU
//of 486DX-33, The DIG requires a 486DX2-66 minimum CPU to run properly.
//
//Older CD-ROM drives may benifit from using updated driver software.  If you are having problems, 
//check with your manufacturer to make sure that you are using the latest drivers for your CD-ROM
//hardware.  This includes drivers for interface cards as well as CD-ROM drives.
//
//Controllers:
//8-bit Future Domain SCSI controller  
//8-Bit Trantor SCSI controller
//8-Bit DTC SCSI controller
//
//CD-ROM drives:
//Media Vision ReNO [SCSI] 
//NEC 210T [SCSI]
//NEC CDR-84-1 [SCSI]
//NEC 3xp [SCSI]
//NEC 3xe [SCSI]
//NEC 4X PRO [SCSI]
//Sony CDU33A-01 [Sony Proprietary Interface]
//Teac CD-55A [Panasonic Proprietary Interface]
//
//Drivers:
//If you are using CORELCDX.COM instead of MSCDEX.EXE you may experience crashes when running The DIG.
//
//
#define TRS_TROUBLE_MICE0   1400
//^f01 Input Device Issues
//^f00
//
//The DIG requires a Microsoft or 100% compatible Microsoft mouse.  We experienced no problems when
//testing The DIG with Microsoft or 100% Microsoft compatible mice.  Use of other types of pointing
//devices is not supported.  If you are using a mouse or other pointing device that is not 100% Microsoft
//compatible you may experience problems.  
//
//
//To use the joystick, it's important to note that you must first calibrate it.  To calibrate the joystick,
//press Ctrl-J while The DIG is running and follow the instructions given.

#define TRS_TROUBLE_EVAL    1500
